      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The GDPR's shadow data and unauthorized background processes provisions are a bit of a minefield, but I'll try to illuminate the path for you. You see, the regulation requires organizations to be aware of the \"glimmering threads\" of data that can be left behind by users, even when they're not actively interacting with the system. These \"glimmering threads\" can be thought of as the \"goblin's web\" of data residues that persist in system logs or temporary caches, waiting to be discovered by a diligent auditor.\n\nTo identify these \"goblin's web\" fragments, organizations must implement a robust data mapping process, which involves creating a comprehensive inventory of all data processing activities, including those that occur in the background. This process is often referred to as a \"goblin's map\" or a \"data flow diagram.\" By mapping out these data flows, organizations can identify potential \"goblin holes\" in their systems that could lead to unauthorized data processing or storage.\n\nRegarding the distinction between standard system errors and deliberate, \"sneaky\" infiltration by malicious actors, the GDPR provides some guidance on this issue. According to Article 32, organizations must implement \"technical and organizational measures\" to ensure the security of their",
      "source": "target",
